Scope notice (please carry this forward)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository does NOT solve, and does not claim to advance, the Clay
Millennium Problem on the Navier-Stokes equations.  It contains no proof of a
finite-time singularity and no proof of global regularity.

Anyone redistributing this work, in whole or in part, is asked to carry this
notice with it, because the results here are easy to overstate if separated
from their stated scope:

  * "Lean-verified" applies only to theorems checked by the Lean kernel with
    no project-specific axioms.  The standard axiom report
    [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound] is the ordinary Lean/mathlib
    foundation and is not a claim of axiom-freedom.
  * "Certificate-verified" applies only to payloads recomputed by the
    independent Python checkers in this repository.  Such results are NOT
    Lean-verified.
  * Several PDE-level conclusions rest on named external classical theorems
    (EXT-P1/P2/P3) that are closed here by AUDITED PAPER PROOFS, not by
    formalisation.  Where a payload records "proved: true" for such a
    theorem, it means exactly that, and every such record also carries
    "lean_formalised: false".
  * Certified time horizons are limits of the proof method.  A certificate
    chain that stops proves nothing about singularities, and its stop time is
    never a singular time of any solution.

The precise, per-result breakdown is in docs/verified_results.md and in the
"Machine-checkable results" section of README.md.

